   "narration": {
    "en": "Two pulses meet on a string. Where they overlap, their displacements simply add: the principle of superposition. A wave and its reflection travelling the opposite way form a standing wave. Some points never move: nodes. Halfway between are antinodes. A string fixed at both ends must have nodes at both ends. So a whole number of half-wavelengths fit: L = n lambda over 2. A pipe closed at one end has a node at the closed end and an antinode at the open end. Only odd harmonics occur. An open pipe has all harmonics. A string 0.5 metre long with wave speed 200 metres per second: fundamental 200 hertz, then 400, 600. A closed pipe 0.25 metre long in air at 340: 340 hertz, then 1020."
   }
